{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<p>A tool is a normal Python function the model may ask us to run. The
<code>@tool</code> decorator turns the function's signature and docstring into the JSON
schema the model sees.</p>

{{ snippet("tools.py", "create_thing") }}

<h2>What the model sees</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Name:</strong> the function name, <code>create_thing</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Description:</strong> the first line of the docstring.</li>
  <li><strong>Parameters:</strong> from the type hints &mdash; <code>str</code> &rarr; string,
      <code>dict[str, str]</code> &rarr; object of strings, <code>list[str]</code> &rarr; array.</li>
  <li><strong>Parameter descriptions:</strong> from the Google-style <code>Args:</code> section.
      These are your best lever for steering the model: say what values mean, give examples.</li>
</ul>

<h2><code>ToolContext</code>: dependency injection</h2>
<p>The model never sees <code>world</code> or <code>log</code>. Parameters typed
<code>ToolContext[T]</code> are hidden from the schema and filled in at runtime by
<em>type</em> from the <code>context=[...]</code> list we pass to the agent loop:</p>
<pre><code>context=[world, log]   # World → ToolContext[World],  TurnLog → ToolContext[TurnLog]</code></pre>
<p>That's how a tool gets the current world without globals. The second context,
<code>TurnLog</code>, collects what happened during this turn:</p>
{{ snippet("tools.py", "turnlog") }}

<div class="box lib">Note the comment in <code>finish()</code>. A <code>ToolContext</code> is a thin
proxy: reading attributes and calling methods reach the real object, but assigning an
attribute on the proxy doesn't. Mutate through methods or mutable containers.</div>

<h2>Errors are feedback</h2>
<p>When a tool raises, the agent loop doesn't crash. It sends
<code>{"error": "..."}</code> back to the model as the tool's result, and the model
usually fixes its next call. So write errors <em>for the model</em>:</p>
{{ snippet("tools.py", "move") }}
<p>Compare <code>"Invalid destination"</code> with
<code>"'the-moon' is not a place. Create it first with create_place."</code> &mdash;
the second one tells the model exactly what to do next.</p>

<h2>Overriding the description</h2>
<p>Sometimes the docstring is for humans and the model should see something shorter:</p>
